{"entity":{"id":"hb:dutch-brazil@1650","name":"Dutch Brazil","dataset":"basemaps","kind":"polity"},"territories":[{"id":5014,"name":"Dutch Brazil","set_id":"hb_1650","class":"dependency","valid_from":1650,"valid_to":1700,"snapshot_held":1,"area_km2":339141.26825800154,"border_precision":3,"capital_name":null,"sovereign":"Netherlands","sovereign_note":"As mapped by Historical Basemaps (world_1650.geojson)","modern_name":"Brazil","min_lon":-59.672701754372014,"min_lat":-3.652157883700966,"max_lon":-45.37131592312696,"max_lat":1.968626819566531,"source_file":"world_1650.geojson","source_row":null},{"id":5611,"name":"Dutch Brazil","set_id":"hb_1700","class":"dependency","valid_from":1700,"valid_to":1715,"snapshot_held":1,"area_km2":339141.26825800154,"border_precision":3,"capital_name":null,"sovereign":"Netherlands","sovereign_note":"As mapped by Historical Basemaps (world_1700.geojson)","modern_name":"Brazil","min_lon":-59.672701754372014,"min_lat":-3.652157883700966,"max_lon":-45.37131592312696,"max_lat":1.968626819566531,"source_file":"world_1700.geojson","source_row":null}],"identities":[{"qid":"Q221357","from_t":1650,"to_t":1654,"method":"name-label+date","confidence":0.95,"label":"Dutch Brazil","description":"Dutch possession in South America between 1630-1654","inception":1630,"dissolved":1654,"enwiki":"Dutch Brazil","summary":"Dutch Brazil (Dutch: Nederlands-Brazilië; Portuguese: Brasil Holandês), also known as New Holland (Dutch: Nieuw-Holland), was a colony of the Dutch Republic in the northeastern portion of modern-day Brazil, controlled from 1630 to 1654 during Dutch colonization of the Americas. The main cities of the colony were the capital Mauritsstad (today part of Recife), Frederikstadt (João Pessoa), Nieuw Amsterdam (Natal), Saint Louis (São Luís), São Cristóvão, Fort Schoonenborch (Fortaleza), Sirinhaém, and Olinda.\nFrom 1630 onward, the Dutch Republic conquered almost half of Brazil's settled European area at the time, with the capital and the Dutch West India Company (GWC) headquarters in Recife. The governor, John Maurice of Nassau, invited artists and scientists to the colony to help promote Brazil and increase immigration."}],"facts":[{"qid":"Q221357","kind":"capital","value_qid":"Q2671184","value_label":"Mauritsstad","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P36","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Mauritsstad"},{"qid":"Q221357","kind":"official_language","value_qid":"Q5146","value_label":"Portuguese","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P37","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Portuguese"},{"qid":"Q221357","kind":"government","value_qid":"Q133156","value_label":"colony","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P122","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"colony"},{"qid":"Q221357","kind":"flag","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Flag of New Holland.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P41","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q221357","kind":"coat","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Coat of arms of Dutch Brazil.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P94","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null}],"people":{},"media":{"Flag of New Holland.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Flag_of_New_Holland.svg/330px-Flag_of_New_Holland.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Holland.svg","author":"Giro720","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Dutch Brazil.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Coat_of_arms_of_Dutch_Brazil.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Dutch_Brazil.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Dutch_Brazil.svg","author":"Samhanin","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}},"names":["Brasil Holandês","Brasil neerlandés","Brasile olandese","Dutch Brazil","Nederlands-Brazilie","Nederlands-Brazilië","Ne